    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A prince-electorate of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> on the Elbe, ruled from 1423 by the Wettin dynasty from Wittenberg and later Dresden.</li><li>Frederick the Wise protected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#martin-luther">Martin Luther</a>, making Saxony the cradle of the Lutheran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>.</li><li>Under Augustus the Strong, King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#poland">Poland</a> from 1697, Dresden became a Baroque capital and Meissen made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a>’s first hard-paste <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#porcelain">porcelain</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Saxony’s Erzgebirge <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silver">silver</a> was struck from about 1500 into some of the first large silver coins of the type later called the thaler, the name that lives on in the word dollar.</p></aside>
    </section>

