    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#roman-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/roman-empire.jpg" alt="Roman Empire" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#roman-empire">Roman Empire</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roman_Empire_Trajan_117AD.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Tataryn, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Electorate of Saxony began as a small territory with a great title. After the old Duchy of Saxony was broken up in 1180, the name and ducal rank passed to the Ascanian dukes, whose line divided into Saxe-Lauenburg and Saxe-Wittenberg. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#golden-bull-of-1356">Golden Bull</a> issued by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#charles-iv">Emperor Charles IV</a> in 1356 settled their dispute over the right to elect the King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#roman-empire">Romans</a> in favour of Duke Rudolf I of Saxe-Wittenberg, making him one of the seven prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#prince-elector">electors</a> and Arch-Marshal of the Empire, with the lands around Wittenberg on the middle Elbe attached to the dignity. When the Ascanian electoral line died out with Albert III in 1422, Emperor Sigismund granted the electorate in 1423 to Frederick the Warlike, Margrave of Meissen of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#house-of-wettin">House of Wettin</a>, as a reward for his support against the Hussites. This joined the electoral title to the much larger and richer Wettin lands of Meissen and, from 1440, Thuringia, and the name Saxony gradually spread to all of them. In the Division of Leipzig of 1485 the brothers Ernest and Albert split the inheritance: Ernest kept the electorate with Wittenberg and most of Thuringia, while Albert received Meissen with Dresden and Leipzig, founding the Albertine line.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#johann-sebastian-bach"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/johann-sebastian-bach.jpg" alt="Johann Sebastian Bach" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#johann-sebastian-bach">Johann Sebastian Bach</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Johann_Sebastian_Bach.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Elias Gottlob Haussmann, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Saxony was one of the most prosperous and urbanised territories of the Empire. Its wealth began with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silver">silver</a> of the Erzgebirge, mined at Freiberg since the twelfth century and, after new strikes from the 1470s, at Schneeberg, Annaberg and Marienberg; the large silver coins struck there from about 1500 were among the first of the type later called the thaler. Mining brought technical expertise, recorded in Georg Agricola’s <em>De re metallica</em> of 1556, and the Freiberg Mining Academy, founded in 1765, became a leading school of mining science. Leipzig grew into a major trade fair city, linking eastern and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a>, and from the seventeenth century it was the centre of the German book trade. Textiles, linen from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#upper-lusatia">Upper Lusatia</a>, glass and, after 1710, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#porcelain">porcelain</a> from the Meissen manufactory developed into important industries, making Saxony a forerunner of German industrialisation. The universities of Leipzig, founded in 1409, and Wittenberg, founded in 1502, made the region a centre of learning. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> Saxony was strictly Lutheran, and the church was organised under consistories controlled by the elector. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#johann-sebastian-bach">Johann Sebastian Bach</a> worked as cantor of St Thomas’s Church in Leipzig from 1723 until his death in 1750, and Dresden became a city of court music, painting and Baroque architecture.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#great-northern-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-northern-war.jpg" alt="Great Northern War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#great-northern-war">Great Northern War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Great_Northern_War_Collage.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Imonoz, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>As a medium-sized power in the heart of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#germany">Germany</a>, Saxony was often drawn into wars it could not control. The Ernestine elector John Frederick I led the Protestant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#schmalkaldic-league">Schmalkaldic League</a>, and his defeat and capture by Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> at the Battle of Mühlberg in 1547 cost his line the electoral title. Maurice of Saxony, who had sided with the emperor and received the electorate, turned against him in the Princes’ Revolt of 1552, forcing the Peace of Passau, and died in 1553 from wounds received at the Battle of Sievershausen. In the Thirty Years’ War John George I first stayed neutral, then joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gustavus-adolphus">Gustavus Adolphus</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sweden">Sweden</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#saxons">Saxon</a> contingent fled at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-breitenfeld-1631">Breitenfeld</a> in 1631, and Saxony suffered heavily from both sides before its separate Peace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#prague">Prague</a> with the emperor in 1635. John <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#george-iii">George III</a> led Saxon troops to the relief of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vienna">Vienna</a> in 1683. Augustus the Strong’s involvement in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#great-northern-war">Great Northern War</a> brought a Swedish invasion and the humiliating Treaty of Altranstädt in 1706. In the Silesian Wars Saxony was defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> at Kesselsdorf in 1745, and in 1756 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#frederick-the-great">Frederick the Great</a> invaded without warning; the Saxon army capitulated at Pirna and was forced into Prussian service, and the country remained occupied for most of the Seven Years’ War.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#augustus-iii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/augustus-iii.jpg" alt="Augustus III" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#augustus-iii">Augustus III</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Louis_de_Silvestre_-_Portrait_of_Augustus_III_of_Poland_(after_1733)_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Louis de Silvestre, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The elector governed as a territorial prince within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, holding a vote in imperial elections and, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>, leading the Protestant estates at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#imperial-diet">Imperial Diet</a>, a role it formally kept as director of the Corpus Evangelicorum even after the conversion of Augustus the Strong. Government developed early into a structured administration: a privy council, a central treasury and, from the sixteenth century, a consistory to govern the church. Elector Augustus (r. 1553–1586) was a notable administrator who reorganised finances and courts, issued the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#saxons">Saxon</a> Constitutions of 1572, a codification of law, and founded the Kunstkammer that grew into Dresden’s state collections. The estates of nobles, prelates and towns met in diets (<em>Landtage</em>) and kept control over taxation, which limited the elector’s absolutism. Junior Albertine lines were endowed in 1656–1657 with the duchies of Saxe-Weissenfels, Saxe-Merseburg and Saxe-Zeitz, which reverted to the electorate when they died out in the eighteenth century. In 1697 Frederick Augustus I, known as Augustus the Strong, converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#catholicism">Catholicism</a> to be elected King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#poland">Poland</a>, beginning a personal union that his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#augustus-iii">Augustus III</a> continued until 1763; the electorate remained Lutheran, guaranteed by the estates. After the Seven Years’ War the <em>Rétablissement</em>, begun under Frederick Christian and continued under Frederick Augustus III, reduced debt, reformed finances and encouraged industry.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#martin-luther"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/martin-luther.jpg" alt="Martin Luther" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#martin-luther">Martin Luther</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lucas_Cranach_d.%C3%84._-_Martin_Luther,_1528_(Veste_Coburg).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Lucas Cranach the Elder, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The electorate’s greatest impact on world history was the protection it gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#martin-luther">Martin Luther</a>. Frederick the Wise, who had founded the university where Luther taught, refused to hand him over after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#diet-of-worms">Diet of Worms</a> in 1521 and hid him in the Wartburg, where Luther translated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#new-testament">New Testament</a>. The Lutheran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> was first institutionalised in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#saxons">Saxon</a> visitations from 1527, which became a model for Protestant churches, and the language of the Saxon chancery, used in Luther’s Bible, contributed greatly to the formation of standard written German. Saxon theologians shaped the Formula of Concord of 1577 and Lutheran orthodoxy. Under Augustus the Strong and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#augustus-iii">Augustus III</a> Dresden became one of the finest Baroque cities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a>, with the Zwinger, the Frauenkirche, the Green Vault treasury and a picture gallery that acquired Raphael’s Sistine Madonna in 1754. At Meissen, Johann Friedrich Böttger and Ehrenfried Walther von Tschirnhaus produced the first European hard-paste <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#porcelain">porcelain</a>, and the manufactory founded in 1710 set a model for royal porcelain works across the continent. Saxony’s industrial and commercial strength later made it one of the most industrialised regions of nineteenth-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#germany">Germany</a>, and today the Free State of Saxony preserves its name, its capital and its collections.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#napoleon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/napoleon.jpg" alt="Napoleon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#napoleon">Napoleon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jacques-Louis_David_-_The_Emperor_Napoleon_in_His_Study_at_the_Tuileries_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jacques-Louis David, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Saxony’s relative decline began in the eighteenth century, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> overtook it as the leading Protestant power of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#germany">Germany</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-poland">Polish crown</a> proved costly: the wars of Augustus the Strong, the expense of royal display and the government of Count Heinrich von Brühl under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#augustus-iii">Augustus III</a> left large debts, and the Seven Years’ War brought occupation, heavy requisitions and the destruction of much of Dresden in the Prussian bombardment of 1760. With the death of Augustus III in 1763 the union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#poland">Poland</a> ended. The <em>Rétablissement</em> restored the finances, and Frederick Augustus III kept Saxony at peace for most of his reign, joining Prussia in the War of the Bavarian Succession of 1778–1779 and declining the Polish crown offered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#constitution-of-3-may-1791">Constitution of 3 May 1791</a>. When war between Prussia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#napoleon">Napoleon</a> broke out in 1806, Saxony sided with Prussia, and its army shared in the defeat at Jena in October 1806. By the Treaty of Posen in December 1806 Frederick Augustus made peace with Napoleon, joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#confederation-of-the-rhine">Confederation of the Rhine</a> and took the title of king, ending the electorate a few months after the dissolution of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>. The Kingdom of Saxony that replaced it lost the northern part of its territory, including Wittenberg, to Prussia in 1815.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Electorate of Saxony was the territory that protected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#martin-luther">Martin Luther</a> and first organised a Lutheran state church; rich from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silver">silver</a> mining and trade, it made Leipzig a centre of commerce and music and Dresden a Baroque capital, before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> overshadowed it in the eighteenth century.</em></p>

